ata in PostGIS\"}],\", or indeed with Postgres’ own \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-geometric.html\",\"children\":\"geometry types\"}],\", you’ve probably also encountered \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/gist-intro.html\",\"children\":\"GiST-based indexes\"}],\". These can handle 2D and higher-dimensional data, enabling speedy queries on proximity, containment, overlap, and so on.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Both index types can work with multiple data items. For example, if you need to find people by their name and date of birth, you can create a multi-column B-Tree index that enables fast searching on the two together.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"But what if you need to search by multiple columns, and the data in those columns is a mix of one- and multi-dimensional? A case in point: what if you need to search simultaneously over space and time? Well, Postgres has you covered here too.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"The TL;DR is: use the \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/btree-gist.html\",\"children\":\"btree_gist\"}],\" extension. Some context and a worked example follow.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"crimes\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Crimes\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#crimes\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Crimes\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"path\",null,{\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"strokeLinecap\":\"square\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"strokeWidth\":1.4,\"d\":\"m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75\"}]}]}]]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"This issue cropped up while I was working with the \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://data.police.uk/data/\",\"children\":\"detailed crime data published by UK police forces\"}],\". The data set has one row per crime: each row has the crime’s type, a latitude and longitude (generalised slightly for anonymity), and the month and year it was reported.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"I needed a big join query to count crimes occurring near millions of specific point-locations at particular times.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"get-the-data\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Get the data\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#get-the-data\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Get the data\",\"children\":\"$L62\"}]]}],\"\\n\",\"$L63\",\"\\n\",\"$L64\",\"\\n\",\"$L65\",\"\\n\",\"$L66\",\"\\n\",\"$L67\",\"\\n\",\"$L68\",\"\\n\",\"$L69\",\"\\n\",\"$L6a\",\"\\n\",\"$L6b\",\"\\n\",\"$L6c\",\"\\n\",\"$L6d\",\"\\n\",\"$L6e\",\"\\n\",\"$L6f\",\"\\n\",\"$L70\",\"\\n\",\"$L71\",\"\\n\",\"$L72\",\"\\n\",\"$L73\",\"\\n\",\"$L74\",\"\\n\",\"$L75\",\"\\n\",\"$L76\",\"\\n\",\"$L77\",\"\\n\",\"$L78\",\"\\n\",\"$L79\",\"\\n\",\"$L7a\",\"\\n\",\"$L7b\",\"\\n\",\"$L7c\",\"\\n\",\"$L7d\",\"\\n\",\"$L7e\",\"\\n\",\"$L7f\",\"\\n\",\"$L80\",\"\\n\",\"$L81\",\"\\n\",\"$L82\",\"\\n\",\"$L83\",\"\\n\",\"$L84\",\"\\n\",\"$L85\",\"\\n\",\"$L86\",\"\\n\",\"$L87\",\"\\n\",\"$L88\",\"\\n\",\"$L89\",\"\\n\",\"$L8a\",\"\\n\",\"$L8b\",\"\\n\",\"$L8c\",\"\\n\",\"$L8d\",\"\\n\",\"$L8e\",\"\\n\",\"$L8f\",\"\\n\",\"$L90\",\"\\n\",\"$L91\"]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"62:[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$58:12:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]\n63:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"The data I used was \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://data.police.uk/data/archive/2017-04.zip\",\"children\":\"the April 2017 archive\"}],\" (which is the latest that goes all the way back to 2010). It’s organised into folders named by year and month (\",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"2017-04\"}],\", \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"2017-03\"}],\", etc.). In each folder there are CSV files for each police force, and for a few different sorts of data. I was interested in street-level crime data, so the files I wanted look like \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"[year-month]/[year-month]-[police-force]-street.csv\"}],\" (for instance, \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"2017-04/2017-04-thames-valley-street.csv\"}],\").\"]}]\n64:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"When I’m exploring CSV data and loading it into Postgres, I generally find \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://github.com/BurntSushi/xsv\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"xsv\"}]}],\" invaluable. Let’s use it to take a look at what we have in these CSVs.\"]}]\n65:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"how-many-crimes-are-recorded\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"How many crimes are recorded?\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#how-many-crimes-are-recorded\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to How many crimes are recorded?\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$58:12:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n67:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"what-do-the-columns-represent\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"What do the columns represent?\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#what-do-the-columns-represent\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to What do the columns represent?\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$58:12:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n69:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"what-are-the-types-of-crime\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"What are the types of crime?\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#what-are-the-types-of-crime\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to What are the types of crime?\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$58:12:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n6a:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Note that I’m using \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"pv\"}],\" with the row count calculated above to monitor progress, and we need the \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"--limit\"}],\" argument because \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"xsv\"}],\" frequency tables default to showing only the"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1," top 10.\"]}]\n6c:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Now let’s load just the subset of data we need into Postgres.\"}]\n6d:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"First, create the schema:\"}]\n6f:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Then, load in the data with \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"COPY … FROM\"}],\". Again I use \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"pv\"}],\" to monitor progress. I use \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"xsv\"}],\" to select specific columns. And I use \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"sed\"}],\" to put the year-month dates into a format Postgres understands, by appending \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"-01\"}],\" (i.e. the first day of the month) to each.\"]}]\n71:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"As quick check that the data have loaded OK, let’s see how crimes are distributed over years with a quick \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tablefunc.html\",\"children\":\"crosstab\"}],\":\"]}]\n73:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"This looks sensible enough. It also tells us that crime classifications changed a few times between 2010 – 2017, which is why some of the categories are a bit oddly overlapping.\"}]\n74:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"make-it-spatial\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Make it spatial\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#make-it-spatial\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Make it spatial\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$58:12:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n75:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"The next step is to create a point geometry out of the latitude and longitude coordinates we’ve been given.\"}]\n76:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"As is annoyingly common, no \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spatial_reference_system\",\"children\":\"coordinate reference system\"}],\" is specified in the data set. The \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://gov.uk/\",\"children\":\"gov.uk\"}],\" standard is British National Grid (BNG). But that’s clearly not what we have here, since that would give us eastings and northings in metres, not latitude and longitude in degrees.\"]}]\n77:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"For now, I’m going to guess we’ve got WGS84, which is the GPS coordinate system (EPSG code 4326). And then I’m going to transform to BNG (EPSG code 27700), because having the coordinates in metres \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://postgis.net/workshops/postgis-intro/geography.html\",\"children\":\"makes distance calculations quick and easy\"}],\".\"]}]\n78:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Two small caveats: (1) I’m using PostGIS’ built-in parametric transformation between the two coordinate systems, which may be off by a few metres. For really precise work, I’d use \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://gis.stackexchange.com/questions/396967/using-ostn15-in-postgis\",\"children\":\"OSTN15\"}],\". And (2) BNG isn’t really appropriate for the Northern Ireland data, but it should still work well enough here.\"]}]\n7a:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"The final step in loading the data is to update table statistics, helping the query planner make good decisions in what follows.\"}]\n7c:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"ask-questions\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Ask questions\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#ask-questions\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Ask questions\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"heig"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"ht\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$58:12:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n7d:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Let’s start by counting crimes within 1km of Stratford station (near the Olympic Park) around the 2012 Olympics in London. Stratford is at grid point E 538566, N 184375, so:\"}]\n7f:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Unsurprisingly, since there were so many more people about, it turns out that crime in these months is a bit higher than usual (the highest July/August crime count in any other year in the data set is 1,430).\"}]\n80:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"It also turns out that this is a pretty slow query, taking about 8 seconds on a fast MacBook Pro. That, of course, is because we haven’t indexed anything.\"}]\n81:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"If we \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"explain analyze\"}],\" the query, there’s a \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"Parallel Seq Scan on crimes\"}],\" in there. It’s nice that Postgres can parallelise it, but it’s still a sequential scan: every row of the database has to be retrieved and checked. Yawn.\"]}]\n83:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"index-on-space\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Index on space\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#index-on-space\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Index on space\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$58:12:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n84:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"The first thing I tried, therefore, was a standard spatial index:\"}]\n86:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"When we now re-run the query, the time drops to 32 milliseconds — about 250 times quicker. An \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"explain analyze\"}],\" shows that a Bitmap Index Scan is being used, which is \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://pganalyze.com/docs/explain/scan-nodes/bitmap-index-scan\",\"children\":\"a kind of middle ground between a sequential and index scan\"}],\".\"]}]\n88:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"This is very much better. But the whole point of this post is that we can do better still.\"}]\n89:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"index-on-space-and-time\",\"classN